**Vulcanisation** (Goodyear, 1839) is the process of heating raw rubber with **sulphur** (or $\mathrm{S_2Cl_2}$, peroxides) at about 100-140 °C, usually with an accelerator (e.g. mercaptobenzothiazole) and activator (ZnO), so that **-S-S- cross-links** form between polyisoprene chains at the C=C / allylic positions, converting soft plastic rubber into a strong, elastic, three-dimensional network.

**How it changes properties.** Raw rubber chains are held only by weak van der Waals forces, so they slip past each other: it is soft and sticky when hot, brittle when cold, and shows permanent set. The sulphur bridges tie chains together, so:
- tensile strength rises (about 10 times) and elasticity improves; no permanent set;
- usable over a wide range (about -40 to 100 °C), not sticky;
- lower water absorption, swelling and solubility in organic solvents;
- better resistance to oxidation, abrasion and wear; better electrical insulation.

**Applications:** tyres and tubes, conveyor belts, shoe soles, hoses, gaskets, rubber bands; high-S rubber (ebonite) for battery cases and insulators.

**Answer: (c) Cross-linked.**

**Justification.** Configuration (tacticity) describes the **spatial arrangement of side groups** about the asymmetric carbons of a chain like $\mathrm{-[CH_2-CHX]_n-}$:
- isotactic: all X on the same side;
- syndiotactic: X alternate regularly;
- atactic: X random.

**Cross-linked** describes the **architecture / shape of the chains** (linear, branched or cross-linked network joined by covalent bridges), not the arrangement of side groups, so it does not belong to the configuration classification.

**Teflon** (polytetrafluoroethylene, $\mathrm{-[CF_2-CF_2]_n-}$) is highly chemical resistant because:
- the **C-F bond is very strong** (about 485 kJ/mol), and the C-C backbone is fully saturated;
- the large F atoms form a **tight sheath around the carbon chain**, shielding it from attack; there is no C-H to be abstracted;
- the chains are linear and highly crystalline, so reagents cannot penetrate.

So it is unaffected even by hot conc. acids, alkalis and aqua regia (only molten alkali metals and $\mathrm{F_2}$ attack it).

Drinking (potable) water (WHO / BIS IS 10500) should be:
- clear, colourless, odourless and pleasant in taste; turbidity below 10 ppm;
- pH 6.5-8.5; TDS below 500 ppm; hardness below about 300 ppm as $\mathrm{CaCO_3}$ (IS 10500: 200 mg/L acceptable); chloride below 250 ppm; fluoride about 1-1.5 ppm;
- free from pathogenic bacteria, toxic metals ($\mathrm{Pb}$, $\mathrm{As}$, $\mathrm{Hg}$) and $\mathrm{H_2S}$, and non-corrosive to pipes.
